  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 300 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nSeasonable temperatures to end the work week:\n\nFollowing the cold frontal passage, temperatures will cool back\nto near normal levels for late August standards. Highs will\nrange from the lower 80s toward central MO, to the lower 90s in\nthe far SW MO and SE KS corners. Lows will also be noticeably\ncooler in the middle 50s to middle 60s. \n\n\nAbnormal temperatures make a comeback for early September:\n\nCome Saturday, though, the ridge currently over the southwest\nCONUS is forecast to begin building northeastward into our area,\nrevitalizing southerly flow and subsequent compressional and\nadvection heating. According to NAEFS and ECMWF ESATs, 99.5th\npercentile 700 mb temperatures will result in above normal\ntemperatures, with the Extreme Forecast Index at values of\n0.8-0.9. Highs are forecast to warm into the 90s, and even near\n100 F, by early next week. Heat Index values will also rise to\n95-105 F, with the greatest values toward the Missouri-Arkansas\nborder. While this level of heat is not crazy for what we've\nalready experienced this August, it is abnormal for early\nSeptember (as highlighted by the ESATs and EFI). The CPC Heat\nIndex climatology/forecast shows 90th percentile Heat Index \nvalues for early September at 95-100 F, with a 70-90% chance to\nexceed that range sometime in early September. So, since 90th\npercentile Heat Index values for early September are between\n95-100 F, a forecast of 100-105 is certainly closer to the\n95th-99th percentile of heat for early September.\n\nWith ridging and high pressure in place, rain chances are\nforecast to be minimal into early next week. That said, global\nmodels and NBM rain chances are quite persistent within\nsouthwesterly flow just north of our area, so it would not be\ntoo out of place to see a few rain chances sneak into the area\nat some points.",
